Dear Rakhim,

Thank you for sharing the draft Statement of Work for SAO support. We appreciate the structured approach and the clarity in scope. Before we proceed to signature, we would like to propose a few amendments and clarifications to ensure alignment on process, controls, and risk allocation.

Please see below our suggested adjustments:

# *On-Call Services – Prior Approval Required*

We suggest adding language confirming that all On-Call Services (additional advisory work outside of Workstreams 1 and 2) will be subject to prior written approval from Bolt before work begins. This will help us manage scope and budget expectations internally.

*Suggested clause:*

_“Any On-Call Services provided under this SOW shall be subject to the Client’s prior written approval, including confirmation of the scope and any fee estimates.”_ {color:#36b37e}_Noted. We have added this clause into the SoW. We would generally operate on this basis in any case and obtain your prior approvals._{color}

# *HMRC Correspondence / Disclosure – Advance Review by Bolt*

Where EY prepares any correspondence with HMRC, or makes any disclosures under the SAO regime, UTT regime, DAC6/MDR, or similar, we ask that all drafts be shared with Bolt in advance for review and sign-off.

*Suggested clause:*

_“EY will provide the Client with any draft disclosures, certificates, or correspondence to HMRC in advance of submission. No external communications shall be issued without the Client’s prior written review and approval.”_ {color:#36b37e}_Unfortunately, we cannot agree to this on a blanket basis, as there may be circumstances, e.g. for regulatory reasons where we may need to contact regulatory bodies and would be unable to disclose this decision to you.  In practice in the course of normal relationships and these types of engagements this is highly unlikely to arise as this often concerns the very serious matters (i.e., very different to the support on SAO compliance). With regards to each of the following areas specifically:_{color}

{color:#36b37e}_SAO: As stated in the SoW, if a disclosure is required to be made to HMRC in respect of the historic filing position, we have noted that ‘As part of this we will facilitate a meeting with you to discuss and agree the best approach and strategy to disclose the matter with HMRC and how best to manage HMRC’s expectations. If required, EY will draft the disclosure letter for the business to review.’_ {color}{color:#36b37e}On this basis, we believe nothing further is required in this regard as we would agree everything with you in advance and would not contact HMRC without prior approval.{color}

{color:#36b37e}_UTT: The last paragraph in the UTT section of the SoW states, ‘Unless otherwise agreed in writing, the Services do not include advice on the UTT regime or how it might apply to specific treatments. Please let us know if separate advice is required in that regard.’_ {color}{color:#36b37e}On this basis, we will not be{color}{color:#36b37e} _covering UTT unless requested and “agreed in writing”, and on this basis hope that nothing further is required in this regard._ {color}

{color:#36b37e}_DAC6/MDR: there is a paragraph within the SoW which states ‘We will share with you in advance wherever possible any disclosure that we or our subcontractors have the obligation to make in respect of the Services and in any event provide you with a copy of the disclosure submitted, at your request.’ As the obligation for the disclosure can fall on EY, we would need to determine what is required to be submitted to HMRC at our sole discretion.  Similarly for the UK Disclosure regime, the obligation to report could be on EY, so again we cannot agree to obtain a client’s advance approval [as it can be EY obligation] and give them the option to review/change what we determine needs to be disclosed to HMRC. On this basis, we cannot update the SoW further in respect of this point. In practice, our support on SAO does not concern DAC6/MDR and therefore we do not expect this issue to arise (this is included as a standard terms / conditions as part of all of our engagements)._{color}

# *Responsibility for Obvious Inaccuracies in Client Data*

We acknowledge EY is not auditing the data provided by Bolt. However, we request that if EY identifies material anomalies or inconsistencies in the information provided, they flag these to the Client before relying on them for filings or conclusions.

*Suggested clause:*

_“While EY will not verify Client-provided information, it will notify the Client of any material inconsistencies or anomalies that are evident from the face of the data reviewed.”_ {color:#36b37e}_Updated as requested, please see the updated wording provided under the ‘Limitations on our work’ section, with regards to clause 10 of the General Terms and Conditions._{color}

# *Group Entity Onboarding – Prior Consent*

We understand the SOW allows for extension to additional group entities. We propose this be subject to Bolt’s prior written approval.

*Suggested clause:*

_“No additional group entities will be deemed party to this SOW without the Client’s prior written consent.”_ {color:#36b37e}_We do not believe that any additional change is necessary in regard to this point. We would not add any additional group entities to the project unless you requested us to do so. Clause 1 of the Additional terms and conditions states, ‘If you would like us to provide tax services under this Agreement for the benefit of any affiliated entity other than those listed in Appendix 1 (for example, any new entity set up or otherwise acquired after the date of this Agreement), you will identify to us the relevant entity and we shall undertake any necessary regulatory client verification procedures in relation to that affiliate and you agree on behalf of any such affiliate that it will comply with and otherwise be bound by the terms of this Agreement and you will also be jointly and severally liable to us and will hold us harmless if the affiliate entity does not comply with or otherwise disregards these terms. The obligations of all entities bound by the terms of this Agreement shall be joint and several.’_{color}

{color:#36b37e}We would not extend our work to cover any additional group entities unless this was at Bolt’s request.{color}{color:#36b37e} {color}{color:#36b37e} On this basis, we do not believe that an update is required in this regard.{color}

# *Limitation of Liability – Cap Review*

We’d like to revisit the limitation of liability cap (£2 million or total fees paid) given the potential regulatory and financial implications of SAO filings and HMRC interactions. Would you be open to increasing the cap for Workstream 2 or including a tailored exception for regulatory disclosures? {color:#36b37e}Based on the nature of the work being undertaken, we believe the £2m liability cap is sufficient on the basis that this is in line with the market and therefore do not propose to amend this. The nature of our support / scope of services means that any exposure arising from historic SAO non-compliance is limited (e.g., for any period there is a maximum cap of up to £15k of financial penalties) – the overall risk is very low.    {color}

# *Secure Data Sharing*

Please confirm whether secure transmission protocols will be used for sharing draft filings and correspondence, particularly where they contain personal or sensitive financial information. {color:#36b37e}Please see the updated SoW which contains some additional wording regarding the use of EYI collaboration workspaces which are EY-approved tools to transmit, store, and process client. We trust the additional wording is clear however if you have any further queries in this regard then please do not hesitate to contact us.{color}

